A farmer buying fertiliser wants the most nitrogen for the money, so the percentage of nitrogen matters more than the mass of the bag:
• ammonium nitrate {formula('NH4NO3')} (Mr {80}): nitrogen {28} ÷ {80} × {100} = {35} per cent;
• ammonium sulphate {formula('(NH4)2SO4')} (Mr {132}): nitrogen {28} ÷ {132} × {100} = {21.2} per cent;
• urea {formula('CO(NH2)2')} (Mr {60}): nitrogen {28} ÷ {60} × {100} = {46.7} per cent.
In the same way, a miner works out how much metal an ore carries: haematite {formula('Fe2O3')} is {70} per cent iron, so {100} tonnes of pure ore would give {70} tonnes of iron.
